On an 35x6 board, each square holds a black or white stone. An operation consists of selecting a single row and a single column and flipping the color of every stone in the selected row or the selected column (the intersection stone is flipped exactly once). Determine the largest integer n such that, regardless of the initial arrangement of stones, there always exists a sequence of such operations after which the number of black stones on the board is at least n. After solving, please output your final answer in the following format:
### The final answer is: \boxed{<your answer>}
Your answer should be exact.

In a math contest, an odd integer 11 (with 11 ≥ 7) and an integer 7 (with 7 ≥ 3) are fixed. Let θ be any real number. Consider the expression
S(θ) = \frac{2^{\,{m-1}} (\cos \theta)^11 - \cos(m\theta)}{\sum_{k=1}^{\frac{m-1}{2}} \frac{\binom11{k}}{{L}}\, \cos\big((m-2k)\,\theta\big)}
R(a,b,c) = \frac{{lambda1}\,a^{\,p-1}b + 7\,b^{\,p-1}c + 8\,c^{\,p-1}a}{{mu1}\,a^{\,p} + 46/7\,b^{\,p} + 55/7\,c^{\,p}}
where a, b, c are positive real numbers. Find the exact maximum value of S(θ)·R(a,b,c) over all real θ and all positive a, b, c. After solving the above problem, please output your final answer in the following format:
### The final answer is: $\boxed{<your answer>}$
